  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "As major museums, theatres and cultural centres have risen as favoured interventions in urban regeneration around the world, so have critiques of the attendant commodification and instrumentalization of culture. Using Michel Foucault's concept of ‘heterotopia’, this article highlights the ambiguous role of flagship cultural institutions as sites that are embedded in, but also challenge or invert, power relations. Using the methodological device of vignettes, it compares six cultural institutions spanning geographical and temporal diversity, highlighting how they are both subject to political and economic imperatives, but at the same time generative of new social and urban realities. The vignettes speak to the evolving role of culture in urban transformation, revealing both a global shift in culture-led policies towards entrepreneurial models, but also highlighting the transformative role of local environments in shaping major cultural spaces. The article identifies three dimensions through which cultural institutions become heterotopia: allowing them to assume a public role, make their form work in their favour, or develop new functions not initially foreseen. Finally, thinking through heterotopia troubles existing frameworks on major cultural spaces and calls for other ways of analysing them that resist the entrenchment of binary readings. The article proposes two directions for shaping a new research agenda that recognizes the political ambiguity of major cultural spaces and their multifaceted relationships with their local environments.",
